From: rhodep port <none@example.com>
Date: Sun, 23 Aug 2026 23:35:00 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] remoteproc: qcom_q6v5: mask the handover IRQ once handover
 has happened

Handover is a one-shot event per start. q6v5_handover_interrupt() does the
work, sets handover_issued and returns, leaving the interrupt enabled for
as long as the remote processor runs, so every further transition on the
line re-enters the handler only to log that it already happened.

On this board the line does keep transitioning. Before the message was
ratelimited it arrived around 26000 times in a boot, and it still comes in
bursts -- 251 suppressed callbacks in one window, from the ADSP, seconds
before an unrelated failure was being chased. Each one is a threaded
interrupt, so each one is a kernel thread wakeup doing nothing.

Nothing above the flag is safe to repeat either: it drops the proxy votes
through q6v5->handover() and clears the interconnect request. Masking the
line after the first one is the honest behaviour.

qcom_q6v5_prepare() enables the interrupt on every start, so the disable
has to happen exactly once per enable. If handover arrived the handler
masks it; if it never did, qcom_q6v5_unprepare() still does, and it now
checks handover_issued to avoid disabling twice and leaving the nesting
count off by one.

Signed-off-by: rhodep port <none@example.com>
---
--- a/drivers/remoteproc/qcom_q6v5.c
+++ b/drivers/remoteproc/qcom_q6v5.c
@@ -78,7 +78,9 @@
  */
 int qcom_q6v5_unprepare(struct qcom_q6v5 *q6v5)
 {
-	disable_irq(q6v5->handover_irq);
+	/* Already masked by the handler if handover arrived */
+	if (!q6v5->handover_issued)
+		disable_irq(q6v5->handover_irq);
 	q6v5_load_state_toggle(q6v5, false);
 
 	/* Disable interconnect vote, in case handover never happened */
@@ -176,6 +178,17 @@
 
 	q6v5->handover_issued = true;
 
+	/*
+	 * Handover happens once per start, and nothing above is safe to run
+	 * twice: it drops the proxy votes and the interconnect request. The
+	 * line is left asserted afterwards on some designs, and with a rising
+	 * edge trigger every further transition re-enters this handler for no
+	 * reason. Mask it here; qcom_q6v5_unprepare() takes care of the case
+	 * where handover never arrived, so the enable in qcom_q6v5_prepare()
+	 * stays balanced either way.
+	 */
+	disable_irq_nosync(q6v5->handover_irq);
+
 	return IRQ_HANDLED;
 }
 
